WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.060
The decision to end this sci-fi cult favorite
after two seasons has already been made, but

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.480
why exactly did Netflix execs decide to pull
the plug on their mind-bending mystery thriller?

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.700
Here are a few reasons why we think The OA
was canceled.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.500
Netflix doesn't release its viewing figures,
so it's impossible to say whether the amount

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.780
of people streaming The OA impacted the decision
to axe it.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.400
What we do know is that the streamer makes
these decisions based on a simple ratio: viewership

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.360
versus cost.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.200
Netflix has confirmed that it adheres to this
formula, and according to The Wrap, an insider

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.660
with knowledge of Netflix's decision to cancel
the drama two seasons in has confirmed that

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.980
The OA fell on the wrong side of the equation.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.880
The OA was "a big creative swing" for the
company, the source said, and while it was

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.400
one that Netflix was "proud to take" it just
wasn't paying off.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:51.460
In June 2019, Netflix chief content officer
Ted Sarandos reportedly ordered dozens of

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.920
his TV and movie execs to rein in their spending.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.960
People familiar with the high-level meeting
told The Information it was made clear that

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:02.100
bigger budget projects needed to bring in
the viewers going forward — building buzz

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.370
and industry credibility was no longer enough.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.680
The Netflix business model relies not only
on the retention of subscribers, but the steady

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.640
influx of new ones, and shows like The OA
don't help in that department.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.560
It was critically acclaimed, yes, but the
Netflix metric deemed that it was no longer

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.060
a draw.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.920
As a report by The Information put it,

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.580
"Netflix has learned that the first two seasons
of a show are key to bringing in subscribers

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.720
— but the third and later seasons don't
do much to retain or win new subscribers.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.080
Ending a show after the second season saves
money, because showrunners who oversee production

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.160
tend to negotiate a boost in pay after two
years."

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:40.600
Understandably, this approach isn't popular
with producers, especially those running shows

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.620
designed to be told over multiple seasons,
like The OA.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.460
One creator of a Netflix show that was axed
after two seasons alleged,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.620
"It's really hard to get [Netflix] to put
money into a third season.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.670
For a creator, you want to be able to reward
your audience with more seasons."

00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:00.240
She's Gotta Have It, American Vandal and Friends
From College, all canceled in 2019, also only

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.140
got two seasons.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.800
Netflix has denied that this is a new policy.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.500
On the surface, The OA is your typical binge-worthy
Netflix show, teasing viewers with glimpses

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.200
of what's to come in order to keep them rooted
in their seats for hours on end.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.659
That relationship between show and viewer
only functions if the build up to those glimpses

00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:22.170
is satisfying enough in its own right, however,
and in the case of The OA, it was very much

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.800
a one-sided relationship.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.920
Simply put, The OA required viewers to sit
through hours of speculative twists and turns

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.320
for ultimately very little payoff in return.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.020
Questions were answered with questions that
lead to yet more questions.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.420
While traditional TV networks may be willing
to churn out endless episodes of a show based

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.630
on mystery alone, much like ABC's Lost, Netflix
isn't going to continue to fund a show that

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.099
loses people's interest before they get to
the payoff.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.740
The OA isn't for everybody.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.220
It's wildly imaginative and incredibly well
executed, but its mysteries are often a bit

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.460
too impenetrable.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.920
Part I ended with a school shooting, thwarted
by, for the sake of clarity, what we'll call

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.200
"interpretive dance."

00:03:01.629 --> 00:03:05.860
One character swallows a sea anemone during
a near-death experience, in the same episode

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.140
in which another character swallows a bird
given to her by an angel with braille on her

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:10.640
face.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.220
The OA Part II is equally weird, and, for
some critics, it just went too far.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.120
According to IndieWire's Ben Travers, Part
II, "doubles down in a longer, more pretentious,

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:24.490
and less vigorous follow-up season that's
part puzzle box, part romance, and wholly

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.260
insane."

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:29.200
If there's one single moment in season two
of The OA that seemed to push people over

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.709
the edge, it's when Brit Marling's "original
angel" meets a telepathic octopus.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.240
Yes, a telepathic octopus.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.780
We had already been through a whole lot of
craziness by the time the show's protagonist

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.280
encounters this creature, whose name, by the
way, is "Old Night."

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.720
"I hold hope for your species."

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.260
"He holds hope for our species."

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:53.660
This extraordinary octopus communicates with
revellers at a packed nightclub via our dimension-traversing

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.080
hero, and it was simply too much for some
viewers.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.560
Even ardent fans of the show were left utterly
perplexed by what they had witnessed, and,

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.739
in true OA fashion, answers weren't exactly
forthcoming.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.640
"What the f--- is this?"

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.980
"I just do lights, bro."

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.540
One of the biggest issues with The OA is that
it seemed to be catering almost exclusively

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:15.540
to a cult audience.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.800
The show built up a dedicated following after
its first season, and rather than trying to

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:24.250
expand that following by making season two
a little more accessible to new viewers, The

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.940
OA seemed to lean even further into the weirdness.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.340
The result was a second season that isolated
anyone that didn't have the patience for telepathic

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:36.441
octopuses and dancing robots, and isolating
potential customers isn't part of the Netflix

00:04:36.441 --> 00:04:37.220
business model.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.080
Could the fact that The OA pandered to its
fandom a little too much have been a factor

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.520
in Netflix pulling the plug?

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.720
According to Slashfilm's Joshua Meyer, the
show seemed to take pleasure in the fact that

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.600
it created an exclusive club for people who
were willing to go along with whatever it

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:51.180
threw at them.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.680
Meyer wrote in his review of The OA Part II,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:58.340
"It gathers fans around itself in a candlelit
attic and seeks to regale them with a tall

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:58.840
tale.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.300
The show has big ideas, but its characters
are all too quick to chase magic mirrors on

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.120
blind faith.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.620
If you're invested in those characters, then
you might be willing to drink from the same

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.120
pitcher of Kool-Aid that they do."

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.220
Could co-creators of The OA Brit Marling and
Zal Batmanglij already be working on another

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.340
project for Netflix?

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.640
Their first show might be done, but the language
that Marling used when she took to Instagram

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.700
following news of The OA's cancellation seemed
to suggest that something else could be in

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:24.440
the pipeline.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.840
The actress and producer posted,

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.100
"We feel profound gratitude to Netflix and
the people we have worked with there for making

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.220
it possible to make Part I and Part II.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.960
While we cannot finish this story, I can promise
you we will tell others."

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.460
Those other stories could well be told via
a different platform, but Netflix's statement

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.800
on the matter also suggested that their relationship
with Marling and Batmanglij wasn't over.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:49.040
Cindy Holland, vice president of original
content for Netflix, said in a statement,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.220
"We are incredibly proud of the 16 mesmerizing
chapters of The OA and are grateful to Brit

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.330
and Zal for sharing their audacious vision.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.110
We look forward to working with them again
in the future, in this and perhaps many other

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:00.920
dimensions."

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.300
There's no denying that Marling is a huge
talent with more to offer, and the "dark flair"

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.660
that Batmanglij brought as director of The
OA didn't go unnoticed.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.200
Here's hoping this daring duo works together
again soon, and gets to see their wild visions

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.040
play out in full.

